---

## 1. Context & Problem Statement

### 1.1. What users experience

Engineers develop jobs locally using their **interactive AD user accounts** and the AD group memberships those accounts already hold. Locally, access works.

When the same job is moved to automation (CI/CD runners, scheduled workers, container workloads), it can no longer run as a person. Corporate policy requires a **system (non-human) account**, and that account’s credentials must live in an **approved safe**—HashiCorp Vault or CyberArk.

### 1.2. Why self-service stops

Both Vault and CyberArk operational models in our enterprise assume an **application-linked** ownership chain: a registered application, an approved safe, CI/operational processes tied to that application, and clear account stewardship.

Many of these workloads are **platform-adjacent or pre-application**: prototypes, internal data workflows, shared pipeline patterns, or teams that do not yet (and may never) own a formal application identity in the IAM/PAM inventory. They therefore:

* Cannot open or operate their own approved safe under current mandates.
* Cannot provision compliant system accounts into a safe they own.
* Still need the same AD group entitlements their interactive user had locally.

**Non-issue:** Password lifecycle for accounts already in an approved safe is solved. Corporate safes rotate on a **90-day** cadence (or lower if configured). Rotation mechanics are not why this ADR exists.

### 1.3. The real burden today (Platform as human broker)

Platform Engineering has absorbed the gap:

1. **Create** static AD system accounts on behalf of requesting teams.
2. **Enroll** those accounts in the **platform-owned** approved safe (because the requester has no application-linked safe).
3. **Map** each account into the **relevant AD groups** the workload needs.
4. **Own** the standing relationship—who asked, which job still needs it, group membership over time, and annual UAR/recertification when teams or pipelines drift or disappear.

This is not a complaint about Vault/CyberArk rotation. It is **policy-compliant automation access without an application-owned safe**, currently delivered as manual platform brokerage (account farming + group mapping under the platform safe).

### 1.4. Why that pattern does not scale

| Pressure | Effect |
| --- | --- |
| No app-linked safe for the caller | Every request lands on Platform to create and hold the account |
| Static account proliferation | One (or more) permanent system accounts per team/workflow under the platform safe |
| AD group mapping brokerage | Platform grants and maintains group membership for workloads it does not run |
| UAR / standing privilege | Annual reviews of system accounts whose real owning team is unclear or gone |
| Permanent directory principals | Accounts persist for as long as someone remembers to keep them; access is not tied to a single job run |

Enterprise Vault **dynamic LDAP** roles (or equivalent CyberArk just-in-time patterns) would address much of this, but are not enabled or not available to this class of caller today. Foundational platform requests that would unlock cleaner PAM paths also face long backlogs.

### 1.5. What we need

A **tactical, policy-aligned** way for authenticated automation identities to obtain **short-lived AD system principals** already placed in required groups—without Platform manually creating static accounts in the platform safe for every team—while preserving a path to native Vault (or CyberArk) dynamic issuance when those capabilities are available to these workloads.

