## 5. Lifecycle, TTL & Credential Handling

| Property | Policy |
| --- | --- |
| Pooling | **None.** Every successful request creates `dyn-svc-[a-z0-9]{8}` (retry on collision). |
| “Single-use” | **Not claimed.** Accounts are non-pooled; the password remains valid for Kerberos/NTLM until `accountExpires` (and residual tickets may last until ticket end—see §8). |
| Default TTL | **4 hours** |
| Max TTL | **24 hours** (hard cap) |
| Password generation | `crypto/rand`, ≥32 chars, mixed classes; UTF-16LE quoted for `unicodePwd` over LDAPS |
| Keytab | Built in-process from the same password + principal (`{sAMAccountName}@{REALM}`); returned as **standard binary keytab, Base64-encoded** in JSON (`keytab_base64`). Prefer AES enctypes (e.g. `aes256-cts-hmac-sha1-96`); include RC4 only if domain policy still requires it. |
| Persistence | Password and keytab material exist in process memory only until the HTTP 201 body is written. **Never** written to DB, cache, KV, logs, traces, or metric labels. |
| Response shape | Always returns `username` + `password` (Vault `/ldap/creds`–shaped) **plus** `keytab_base64` and `principal` for Kerberos clients. Keytab is an extension beyond stock Vault LDAP responses—clients that only need password may ignore it. |

Example `201` body:

```json
{
  "username": "dyn-svc-7e2a1b9c",
  "password": "…",
  "principal": "dyn-svc-7e2a1b9c@CORP.EXAMPLE.COM",
  "keytab_base64": "BQIAAAB…",
  "lease_duration": 14400,
  "account_expires_utc": "2026-10-09T15:13:00Z"
}
```

Clients decode once to a file mode `0600` (e.g. `kinit -kt` / Spark `--keytab`). Treat `keytab_base64` as **equivalent secret material to the password** (same keys).

Optional hardening (preferred where the platform supports it): **response wrapping** or a one-shot retrieval token so the long-lived pipeline log stream never holds the raw password or keytab.

